SELECT 'bd3d07f9-6c76-77d1-b5f5-ec478c69d209', 'ff03cd6f-75e5-7cb9-880d-0e6e57fe44d4', 'Infix, Postfix, Prefix', 'Infix, Postfix, Prefix', 'Humans write x + y; machines prefer x y + or + x y, because those forms need no brackets, no precedence table and no associativity rules — a single left-to-right pass with a stack evaluates them. Converting between the three notations is the classic stack algorithm.
Infix / Postfix / Prefix — the three ways to place an operator relative to its operands: infix between them (x + y), postfix after them (x y +, also called Reverse Polish Notation), prefix before them (+ x y, Polish Notation).
Infix	Postfix	Prefix
x + y	x y +	+ x y
x + y * z	x y z * +	+ x * y z
(x + y) * z	x y + z *	* + x y z
a + b * (c - d) / e	a b c d - * e / +	+ a / * b - c d e
a ^ b ^ c (right-assoc)	a b c ^ ^	^ a ^ b c
Why postfix and prefix are useful
No parentheses
The position of each operator already says which operands it applies to, so brackets are never needed. (x + y) * z is just x y + z *.
No precedence or associativity rules
Those rules only exist to disambiguate infix. Once an expression is in postfix or prefix, * before + is decided by position, not by a table.
One pass to evaluate
A single left-to-right scan with one stack evaluates postfix (right-to-left for prefix). Compilers and calculators (HP''s RPN models, the JVM''s stack machine, PostScript) run on exactly this.
Same tree, three traversals
All three notations are traversals of the same expression tree: prefix = preorder, infix = inorder, postfix = postorder. Converting is just re-traversing.
   +                    preorder  (prefix)  : + a * b c
 /   \                  inorder   (infix)   : a + b * c
a     *                 postorder (postfix) : a b c * +
     / \
    b   c
Precedence and associativity
Infix is ambiguous without two extra rules. Precedence decides which operator binds tighter when they differ; associativity decides the order when they are equal.
Operator	Precedence	Associativity	Example
^	3 (highest)	right to left	2 ^ 1 ^ 2 = 2 ^ (1 ^ 2) = 2 ^ 1 = 2
*, /	2	left to right	12 / 2 * 3 = (12 / 2) * 3 = 18
+, -	1 (lowest)	left to right	10 + 2 - 3 = (10 + 2) - 3 = 9
Precedence: 10 + 20 * 2 → 10 + 40 → 50, not 60.
Left associativity: 10 - 2 - 3 → (10 - 2) - 3 → 5, not 11.
Right associativity: 2 ^ 3 ^ 2 → 2 ^ (3 ^ 2) → 2 ^ 9 → 512, not 64.
How the rules enter the algorithm
In the conversion algorithms, precedence decides whether an incoming operator pops the stack top; associativity decides what happens when they are equal: left-associative operators pop the equal top (so the earlier one is output first), right-associative ones do not.
Converting by hand: fully parenthesise, then move operators
Parenthesise fully
Add brackets that make precedence and associativity explicit: x + y * z → (x + (y * z)).
Convert innermost first
Replace each (a op b) by a b op (postfix) or op a b (prefix), working outwards: (x + (y z *)) → x y z * +.
Drop the brackets
They are no longer needed — the result is the postfix (or prefix) form.
Infix	Fully parenthesised	Postfix	Prefix
x + y * z	(x + (y * z))	x y z * +	+ x * y z
(x + y) * z	((x + y) * z)	x y + z *	* + x y z
a - b - c	((a - b) - c)	a b - c -	- - a b c
a ^ b ^ c	(a ^ (b ^ c))	a b c ^ ^	^ a ^ b c
Infix to postfix (shunting-yard algorithm)
Example
Input: a+b*c
Output: abc*+
Input: (a+b)*c
Output: ab+c*
Input: a+b*(c-d)/e
Output: abcd-*e/+
Idea
Operands go straight to the output; they are already in the right order. Operators wait on a stack until every operator that must be applied before them has been output. An operator on the stack must come out before an incoming operator of lower (or equal, for left-associative) precedence — that is the only rule. Brackets are a fence: ( is pushed and nothing below it can be popped until the matching ) arrives.
Operand
Append it to the output.
(
Push it.
)
Pop and output operators until the (; pop the ( and discard it (brackets never appear in postfix).
Operator x
While the stack top is an operator with higher precedence than x, or equal precedence and x is left-associative, pop it to the output. Then push x.
End of input
Pop and output everything left on the stack.
Symbol	Action	Stack (bottom → top)	Output
a	operand → output		a
+	stack empty → push	+	a
b	operand → output	+	a b
*	higher than + → push	+ *	a b
(	push	+ * (	a b
c	operand → output	+ * (	a b c
-	top is ( → push	+ * ( -	a b c
d	operand → output	+ * ( -	a b c d
)	pop to (: output -, discard (	+ *	a b c d -
/	equal to *, left-assoc → pop *; + is lower → stop; push /	+ /	a b c d - *
e	operand → output	+ /	a b c d - * e
end	pop everything		a b c d - * e / +
PREC = {''+'': 1, ''-'': 1, ''*'': 2, ''/'': 2, ''^'': 3}
RIGHT_ASSOC = {''^''}

def infix_to_postfix(expr):
    out, st = [], []
    for ch in expr:
        if ch == '' '':
            continue
        if ch.isalnum():                       # operand: straight to the output
            out.append(ch)
        elif ch == ''('':
            st.append(ch)
        elif ch == '')'':
            while st[-1] != ''('':               # pop until the matching ''(''
                out.append(st.pop())
            st.pop()                           # discard the ''('' itself
        else:                                  # operator
            while (st and st[-1] != ''('' and
                   (PREC[st[-1]] > PREC[ch] or
                    (PREC[st[-1]] == PREC[ch] and ch not in RIGHT_ASSOC))):
                out.append(st.pop())           # higher (or equal, left-assoc) comes out first
            st.append(ch)
    while st:
        out.append(st.pop())
    return ''''.join(out)

for e in ["a+b*c", "(a+b)*c", "a+b*(c-d)/e", "a^b^c", "a-b-c"]:
    print(e, "→", infix_to_postfix(e))
#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <stack>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int prec(char op) {
    if (op == ''^'') return 3;
    if (op == ''*'' || op == ''/'') return 2;
    if (op == ''+'' || op == ''-'') return 1;
    return 0;
}
bool rightAssoc(char op) { return op == ''^''; }

string infixToPostfix(const string& expr) {
    string out;
    stack<char> st;
    for (char ch : expr) {
        if (ch == '' '') continue;
        if (isalnum(static_cast<unsigned char>(ch))) {
            out += ch;                                   // operand: straight to the output
        } else if (ch == ''('') {
            st.push(ch);
        } else if (ch == '')'') {
            while (st.top() != ''('') { out += st.top(); st.pop(); }   // pop until the matching ''(''
            st.pop();                                    // discard the ''('' itself
        } else {
            while (!st.empty() && st.top() != ''('' &&
                   (prec(st.top()) > prec(ch) || (prec(st.top()) == prec(ch) && !rightAssoc(ch)))) {
                out += st.top(); st.pop();               // higher (or equal, left-assoc) comes out first
            }
            st.push(ch);
        }
    }
    while (!st.empty()) { out += st.top(); st.pop(); }
    return out;
}

int main() {
    for (string e : {"a+b*c", "(a+b)*c", "a+b*(c-d)/e", "a^b^c", "a-b-c"})
        cout << e << " → " << infixToPostfix(e) << "\n";
}
