  ('ba717e1b-9583-7378-9dc7-d4646e489f76', 'b0Y', 'callout', '{"variant":"warning","title":"Depth limits","doc":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each language limits recursion depth: Python stops at about "},{"type":"text","text":"1000","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" frames ("},{"type":"text","text":"sys.setrecursionlimit","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" raises it, but the C stack still overflows around tens of thousands), Java and C++ overflow their thread stack at roughly "},{"type":"text","text":"10^4","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"–"},{"type":"text","text":"10^5","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" frames depending on frame size, Node around "},{"type":"text","text":"10^4","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". A linear recursion over "},{"type":"text","text":"n = 10^5","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" elements must be rewritten as a loop or use an explicit stack."}]}]}}'),
  ('9090179e-9de2-726d-bf5e-84718f13158f', 'b0Z', 'heading', '{"level":1,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Interview Q&A"}]}'),
  ('3b845ed8-fdc6-70de-8c54-5f06f82cbb53', 'b0a', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What are the two things every recursive function needs?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"text","text":"base case","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that returns without recursing, and a "},{"type":"text","text":"recursive case","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" whose argument is strictly closer to the base case. Missing or unreachable base cases give infinite recursion; a recursive case that does not shrink the input (e.g. "},{"type":"text","text":"f(n)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" calling "},{"type":"text","text":"f(n)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":") does the same."}]}]}}'),
  ('fd848963-86b5-7f0b-ad36-2505015fc3ea', 'b0b', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is tail recursion, and does it help?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A call is tail-recursive when the recursive call is the very last action, with nothing left to do after it returns — "},{"type":"text","text":"jos","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" above is "},{"type":"text","text":"not","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", because it still adds "},{"type":"text","text":"k","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and takes "},{"type":"text","text":"% n","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" after the call returns. C++ compilers optimise tail calls at "},{"type":"text","text":"-O2","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; Python and Java never do, JavaScript engines in practice do not either. So in those languages tail recursion is a style, not a space saving — convert to a loop when depth matters."}]}]}}'),
  ('f11afadc-2d0f-7d5d-b603-1efbd3db23c2', 'b0c', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why is Tower of Hanoi O(2^n) and not O(n)? There is only one print per call."}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Because there are "},{"type":"text","text":"2^n − 1","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" calls that print: "},{"type":"text","text":"moves(n) = 2·moves(n−1) + 1","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":". The recursion tree is a full binary tree of height "},{"type":"text","text":"n","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and every node does "},{"type":"text","text":"O(1)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" work, so the total is the number of nodes. A single "},{"type":"text","text":"print","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" per call does not mean a linear number of calls."}]}]}}'),
  ('c7506962-d797-7ed1-8bf2-5edacbfe5d2e', 'b0d', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do you turn an exponential recursion into a fast one?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check whether the same subproblem appears many times in the tree (rope cutting: "},{"type":"text","text":"maxPieces(2)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is recomputed; subset sum: "},{"type":"text","text":"(n, target)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" repeats). If so, store each result the first time it is computed — "},{"type":"text","text":"memoisation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (top-down DP) — or fill a table from the base cases up (bottom-up DP). If every leaf is a distinct required answer (subsets, permutations, Hanoi) the output itself is exponential and no algorithm can be faster."}]}]}}'),
  ('b160bfbd-9b9a-7cff-ba95-0806794146f2', 'b0e', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recursion vs iteration — when to pick which?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recursion wins when the problem is naturally a tree of choices (subsets, permutations, Hanoi, tree traversals): the call stack keeps the partial state for you and the code mirrors the definition. Iteration wins for linear problems (sum, factorial, Josephus) where recursion only adds "},{"type":"text","text":"O(n)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" stack and the risk of overflow. Any recursion can be made iterative with an explicit stack; it is just more code."}]}]}}'),
  ('036c7c85-8a73-7908-bb30-c6ef101b5fbc', 'b0f', 'question', '{"prompt":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What does the Josephus formula (jos(n-1, k) + k) % n actually mean?"}]}]},"answer":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"After the first kill the circle is one person smaller and the count restarts at position "},{"type":"text","text":"k","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (0-based). Renumber that person as "},{"type":"text","text":"0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and the rest is a smaller instance of the game. Its survivor "},{"type":"text","text":"j","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" corresponds to "},{"type":"text","text":"j + k","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in the original numbering — the "},{"type":"text","text":"% n","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" wraps around the circle. Unwinding from "},{"type":"text","text":"jos(1) = 0","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" gives the answer in "},{"type":"text","text":"O(n)","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with no simulation."}]}]}}')
